---
import { formats } from "../lib/colors.js";
---

<template id="guideTemplate">
  <section class="guide-start">
    <h3>Start with a few colors</h3>
    <p>
      Paste values into Color input, or choose Load sample. The preview updates
      as you type. Try these three together.
    </p>
    <div class="guide-example">
      {["#D95D39", "#F3DFA2", "#437F78"].map((c) => (
        <code>
          <i style={"background:" + c}></i>
          {c}
        </code>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
  <details open>
    <summary>Importing colors</summary>
    <p>
      Auto-detect accepts HEX, RGB, RGBA, HSL, and OKLCH together. Separate
      entries with spaces, commas, newlines, semicolons, or pipes. For a list
      like the one below, choose Custom and enter <code>::</code>.
    </p>
    <pre>#f80 :: rgb(10, 90, 80) :: #345</pre>
    <p>
      Spaces and commas inside a color function stay together. Check the message
      below the editor for any unrecognized entries.
    </p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>Working with groups</summary>
    <p>
      Use Single Group for one palette. Switch to Multi Group to compare up to
      four palettes side by side or stacked on mobile. Select a group tab before
      editing, copying, or exporting. The preview shows up to 250 colors across
      the visible groups.
    </p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>Choosing a layout</summary>
    <p>
      Grids, tiles, and circles help you inspect individual colors. Stripes and
      diagonal bands show neighboring colors edge to edge. Radial arranges them
      around a wheel.
    </p>
    <div class="guide-layouts" aria-hidden="true">
      <i></i>
      <i></i>
      <i></i>
    </div>
    <p>
      Typography repeats one bold sentence for each color. Scroll sideways to
      read the complete line on a narrow screen.
    </p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>Interactive and Visualize Only</summary>
    <p>
      In Interactive mode, select a swatch to edit it. Open Visualization
      settings to choose which labels appear. Switch to Visualize Only for an
      uninterrupted view of the palette. Both modes work in fullscreen.
    </p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>Editing and organizing</summary>
    <p>
      Select a swatch, adjust the picker or sliders, and choose Apply. You can
      also enter a color value or give it a name. The ≈ symbol marks an
      approximate color name.
    </p>
    <p>
      Drag swatches to reorder them, or use Move earlier and Move later in the
      editor. Visualization settings includes sorting, reversing, and a hue
      shift for the entire active group.
    </p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>Copying, exporting, and sharing</summary>
    <p>
      Copy an individual value from the swatch editor. Copy all takes the active
      group, using the output format and separator in Visualization settings.
    </p>
    <p>
      Export a preview as PNG or SVG, or palette data as CSS, JSON, or TXT.
      Share copies a link to the whole workspace. Large workspaces can travel as
      a file and be restored through Export.
    </p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>Shortcuts and tips</summary>
    <p>
      <kbd>Tab</kbd> moves between controls. Press <kbd>Enter</kbd> on a swatch
      to edit it, or <kbd>Esc</kbd> to close a dialog. Use <kbd>Ctrl / ⌘</kbd> +{" "}
      <kbd>Z</kbd> to undo and add <kbd>Shift</kbd> to redo.
    </p>
    <p>
      Your workspace is saved in this browser. To start with a specific input
      format, open its visualizer below.
    </p>
    <nav class="formats" aria-label="Format-specific visualizers">
      {formats.map((f) => (
        <a href={"/" + f.toLowerCase() + "-visualizer/"}>{f}</a>
      ))}
    </nav>
  </details>
</template>
